with rise of iron destiny feels like a  game that's within reach of fresh ideas  but can't quite escape its own past  there are moments of exhilaration  throughout the newest expansion and a  few inspiring missions remain rooted in  my memory but as Bungie shooter Wade's  into its third year a sense of fatigue  has risen to the surface  Destiny's exhaustion is beginning to  show this may not make sense to you but  it took your fight to remind me that  immortality is not the same as  invincibility from a story standpoint  rise of iron follows our Guardians as  they assist lord saladin one of the last  remaining members of the iron Lords and  his fight against Siva a nanobot plague  that all but exterminated his group  years before present events at its  outset rise of iron displays a confident  sense of character with Lord of the  Rings inspirations in game of throne  aesthetics to boo but that wears thin  soon thereafter Destiny's new campaign  lasts for about 90 minutes and while it  does a decent job of setting up the  expansions late-game missions and public  events the plot serves only to introduce  us to new quest givers in areas of  interests any narrative promised the  opening moments displays soon fades the  campaign's final boss battle may be  thrilling as it encourages frantic melee  combat against an onslaught of enemies  but the short trip it takes to get there  as forgettable as a major destiny  expansion though rise of iron also  brings additional cooperative strikes  public events player-versus-player  options a new social space a new patrol  zone and a six player raid but much of  rise of iron's content is taken directly  from Destiny's first two years and given  a fresh coat of paint  yes Siva infected enemies bring new  dangers to firefights and existing  bosses have changed their combat  strategy to make things feel just fresh  enough but the destiny grind is more  apparent here than ever  I'm waiting through strikes I've played  dozens of times before the wretched I  strike rise of iron truly new  cooperative venture is one of the  aforementioned shining moments but it's  surrounded by vapid echoes of the past  supremacy rise of iron's player versus  player content follows suit the new  supremacy game-mode requires you to pick  up fallen orbs from slain enemies not  granting you full credit for a kill  until you do so their glory goes while  supremacy does lure you out of your  comfort zone with enticing scenarios the  experience is only a slight variation on  the classic deathmatch formula as with  rise of iron's campaign supremacy feels  uninspired rise of iron's maps are well  designed combining close quarters  battles and long range engagements with  seeming ease but these maps just set the  stage for a new mode that doesn't pull  its own creative weight remind me never  to take you lightly Guardian Double Down  you  shame  yes three down look at them fools I  can't believe what I'm seeing  rise of iron's redundant content is a  shame too considering the moments of  greatness that it does deliver the  questline to obtain the newest version  of Gjallarhorn destinies now mythical  rocket launcher culminates a massive  battle the likes of which Bungie hasn't  created since its Halo days archons  forged the expansion's new public event  space plays host to chaotic battles  between a possible nine players in a  variety of alien bosses the sheer  spectacle of these skirmishes combines  with Destiny's mechanical fluidity to  create some of the shooters finest  moments to date this rings true in rise  of iron's wrath of the Machine raid the  trek pits you and five other players  against an array of enemies and a bevy  of complex scenarios that don't use boss  battles and hordes of enemies just as  crutches  while these elements are present rather  the Machine demands that you rethink  what a boss fight can be without  spoiling too much one section of the  raid asks you not to just battle a  massive enemy but use it as  transportation to wrath of the machines  final encounter is also one of those  shining moments I keep coming back to  despite the difficulty you'll encounter  it's a stunning combination of teamwork  survival techniques and combat tactics  as well as using the expansions  pervasive seva against itself that will  carry your squad through but the raid is  the sparkling light at the end of an  often dreary tunnel I spent dozens of  hours slogging through familiar strikes  uninspired new crucible content and a  patrol zone that felt like a retread not  an adventure I found myself playing more  for the new gear and light levels for  the progress that is than the actual  enjoyment  approach as an expansion that revamped  how destiny actually played last years  the taken King was a major step forward  for the shooter but rise of iron feels  just like the other foot catching up to  rest firmly in place it feels timid it  feels safe  it feels like the last remaining breath  before the possible sequel as if destiny  is standing still and waiting for  inspiration to arrive rather than going  out to find it the iron Lords are gone  but our fight is far from over  [Music]  [Music]
 

